# Rust Language Support Guide

This guide provides information on how to interact with Rust functionality using Lux.

## Setting Up the Rust Module

To set up and use the Rust module in Lux, follow these steps:

1. Ensure you have Rust installed on your system.
2. Add the necessary dependencies to your `mix.exs` file.
3. Configure the Rust module in your project.

Example `mix.exs` configuration:
defmodule YourProject.MixProject do
  use Mix.Project

  def project do
    [
      app: :your_project,
      version: "0.1.0",
      elixir: "~> 1.12",
      start_permanent: Mix.env() == :prod,
      deps: deps()
    ]
  end

  defp deps do
    [
      {:rustler, "~> 0.28"}
    ]
  end
end

## Running Rust Code

To run Rust code, use the `run_rust_code/1` function from the `Lux.Rust` module.

defmodule Lux.Rust do
  @moduledoc """
  Module for interacting with Rust functionality.
  """

  def run_rust_code(code) do
    # Placeholder implementation
    IO.puts("Running Rust code: #{code}")
  end
end

## Configuring Cache Directory

To configure the cache directory, use the `cache_dir/1` function from the `Lux.Rust` module.

defmodule Lux.Rust do
  @moduledoc """
  Module for interacting with Rust functionality.
  """

  def run_rust_code(code) do
    # New implementation using Rust module
    Rust.run(code)
  end

  def cache_dir(path) do
    # New implementation using Rust module
    Rust.set_cache_dir(path)
  end

  def prism_enabled?() do
    # Check if Prism functionality is enabled
    Application.get_env(:your_project, :prism_enabled, false)
  end

  def run_prism_code(code) do
    if prism_enabled?() do
      Rust.prism_run(code)
    else
      IO.puts("Prism functionality is disabled.")
    end
  end
end

Example usage:

Lux.Rust.run_rust_code("println!(\"Hello, world!\");")
Lux.Rust.cache_dir("/path/to/cache/dir")
